{"connection_id": "BC-ea7d18648fc1cfd21b73", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.5", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "abiding produces fruit", "note": "The commentary says good work grows from a cultivated field and leaves a remainder; Jesus says those who abide in him bear much fruit, making lasting yield depend on remaining in him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-43f3a93a5f1d210fc488", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.6", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fruitless branches wither and burn", "note": "The commentary links dried growth to loss and fire to two outcomes; Jesus describes an unfruitful branch drying up and being thrown into the fire, a specific but differently framed plant-and-fire image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80674b8aa844c10e7f43", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.6", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Unfruitful branch dries and is thrown into fire", "note": "A branch not abiding is dried, gathered, and burned, a concentrated Christian image for the commentary's dried vegetation and fire-bound outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-75bff9e2c8a06df4c59f", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.7", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "words abiding", "note": "The commentary links the remembered word with lasting good; Jesus says his words abide in disciples who abide in him, joining remembered instruction to the fruit-bearing image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7175ef70b023987d2962", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.24", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing the word passes from death into life", "note": "The one who hears and believes has eternal life and has crossed from death to life, a Christian response-to-reminder counterpart to the commentary's fire-bound non-life and purified alternative."}
{"connection_id": "BC-75699fedc6f03475bd36", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.46", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jesus says Moses wrote of him", "note": "Jesus grounds his appeal in Moses's writing, a Christian interpretation of Mosaic pages that belongs beside the commentary's appeal to Moses as an earlier written authority."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11d3345d588b92628795", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.8.12", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Following the light on the way to life", "note": "Jesus promises that followers of the world's light will not walk in darkness but have life's light; it is a Christian path-and-light counterpart to the commentary's fire sought for guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-246f3ea5d4b4da040565", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jude.1.13", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sea waves cast up foam", "note": "The commentary compares flood foam with the refuse of vegetation and contrasts it with useful remainder; Jude calls false teachers wild sea waves foaming up their shame, giving foam a specific image of worthless discharge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c87be7a03d13ecd3d738", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jude.1.23", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Saving someone by snatching them from fire", "note": "Jude distinguishes rescuing people from fire from sharing their defilement, a concrete Christian complement to the commentary's concern with approaching versus entering fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1beefc99c31ed63b7e1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.21", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rich toward God", "note": "The commentary contrasts preferred worldly life with the lasting hereafter; Jesus ends the rich fool's story by contrasting stored earthly goods with being rich toward God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-60199e4071df712ff69f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.16.25", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Present goods reversed into future comfort and anguish", "note": "Abraham contrasts the rich man's received good things in life with his later anguish and Lazarus's comfort, closely framing the commentary's worldly preference and later permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-33190dc22ca69e54a787", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.22.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A rite performed in remembrance", "note": "Jesus commands the meal to be done in his remembrance; its explicit liturgical remembering is a Christian parallel to the commentary's fire-side prayer for remembrance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1cc55e23ad08786005a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word as seed", "note": "The commentary moves from recited revelation to pasture and a field of outcomes; Jesus identifies the seed as God's word, joining the reminder and agricultural imagery in one interpretation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ed7021f79bc68bc50461", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.8.15", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing, holding fast, bearing fruit", "note": "The commentary divides hearers and describes cultivated land yielding a lasting share; Jesus says the good soil hears the word, holds it fast, and bears fruit with endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1e22a9af0e1445c17e9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.12.26", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "The book of Moses at the bush", "note": "Jesus identifies the bush episode as a passage in the book of Moses, directly joining the commentary's final Moses pages to its opening fire encounter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39af90a429131e0e7dcd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.13.32", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The day and hour known only to the Father", "note": "Jesus says the decisive day and hour are known to the Father alone, a close Christian analogue to the commentary's divinely concealed hour."}

