{"connection_id": "BC-0735e7a11e6e35265c4b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.40", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Weeds gathered and burned at the age's end", "note": "Jesus explains the weed-and-fire image as an end-time sorting, a necessary interpretive continuation of the field scene and the commentary's future fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a3e136ea4e257f47148", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.43", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Righteous shining in their Father's kingdom", "note": "The righteous shine in the kingdom after the furnace judgment, a Christian continuation that meets the commentary's high degrees and lasting reward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ed3c6c053e3b2a07d875", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.46", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eternal punishment and eternal life", "note": "The commentary sets the one in fire against the one purified and praying; Jesus' judgment scene ends with two groups sent to eternal punishment or eternal life, a direct afterlife counterpart to the divided fates."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2908de12400a80df5a68", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.46", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Eternal punishment and eternal life", "note": "The final separation gives the two groups eternal punishment or eternal life, a Christian counterpart to the commentary's split between fire and the purified, praying person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c2bb3f2c6f943b5382e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.3.12", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wheat gathered and chaff burned with unquenchable fire", "note": "The coming judge stores wheat but burns chaff, tightly combining the commentary's useful remainder, agricultural waste, human division, and great fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb673d47d7726009c9b2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure on earth", "note": "The commentary says people put worldly life first though another life lasts; Jesus warns against storing treasure on earth, making present attachment the explicit choice at issue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2493768d18b95e997f84", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.20", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure in heaven", "note": "The commentary calls the hereafter better and more abiding; Jesus directs disciples to store treasure in heaven, giving a concrete alternative to earthly preference."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72d2ef1543867c5cf471", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.6", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Prayer to the Father who sees in secret", "note": "Jesus places prayer in the hidden room before the Father who sees in secret, directly combining the commentary's prayer-for-remembrance with its hidden divine knowledge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-814f54a70c8c8747f8c2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.9", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Prayer that hallows God's name", "note": "The opening petition of the Lord's Prayer hallows the Father's name, an explicit Christian prayer counterpart to the commentary's remembering the Lord's name and praying."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c81ff8ab3f7b65f91cb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Broad way leading to destruction", "note": "The broad gate and way end in destruction, providing a Christian path-and-outcome counterpart to the commentary's guidance, choice, and fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf81b6fb18dbaa9e1b59", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Narrow way leading to life", "note": "The difficult way leads to life and few find it, sharpening the commentary's sought guidance and its contrast between life-oriented response and loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1c9e143ad6e06b49b71", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.24", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing and doing the words", "note": "The commentary says the reminder divides people by their response; Jesus compares the one who hears and does his words to a builder, tying reception of instruction to the outcome of a house."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d5f7ec30844269a3f34c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.24", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing and doing words builds on rock", "note": "The person who hears Jesus's words and does them is likened to a wise builder, a Christian response-to-reminder counterpart to the commentary's beneficial admonition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d79bb03b5eda55a5087", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.25", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flood cannot move the house", "note": "The commentary's flood carries off refuse while useful matter remains; the house built on rock stands through rain, flood, and wind, a close counter-image of what endures the waters."}
{"connection_id": "BC-247efd4fa739c2a928d9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.25", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rain and floods fail to overthrow the founded house", "note": "Rain, flood, and wind strike the rock-founded house but it remains, directly meeting the commentary's flood and the contrast between what goes away and what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5973e8df5d6f7980fb9a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.26", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing without doing", "note": "The commentary distinguishes those who remember from those who avoid the reminder; Jesus contrasts the hearer who does not act with the wise builder, specifying failure to act as the decisive difference."}
{"connection_id": "BC-733dd7ebd53de220662a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.26", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hearing without doing builds on sand", "note": "The hearer who does not act on the words builds on sand, providing a counterpart to the commentary's person who avoids the reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6948adee3fc6ebd432d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.27", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flood exposes the failed foundation", "note": "The commentary's flood removes what cannot last; Jesus' flood causes the ungrounded house to collapse, dramatizing the consequence of rejecting the words heard."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db0a06cc8a20b440ad45", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.27", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Flood destroys the house with no foundation", "note": "Rain and floods bring a great fall to the sand-founded house, completing the commentary's water-borne testing and opposed human outcomes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e49794a589437a67e799", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.10", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Every knee bends at the exalted name", "note": "The universal bending of knees at the name gives a Christian liturgical analogue to the commentary's prostration and exalted name, with a different christological setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eadfa8d47bfa18928967", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.9", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A name highly exalted above every name", "note": "God highly exalts a name above every name, a Christian reapplication of name-and-highness that is relevant to the commentary's praise of the highest Lord but does not share its speaker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cf5450ef5e1a08c249d0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.15.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The song of Moses as praise", "note": "The heavenly worshippers sing the song of Moses in praise of God's great works, a Christian liturgical meeting of Moses, praise, and enduring divine rule."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db278f1d802fd2fc8160", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Lake of fire called the second death", "note": "Revelation names the fiery lake the second death; its wording supplies a deliberate Christian contrast to the commentary's fire in which death and life are both denied."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6484c97c0836cebcdda7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.21.4", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Future with death abolished", "note": "Revelation's renewed world abolishes death, mourning, and pain; its eschatological endpoint gives a Christian counter-horizon to the commentary's fire where neither death nor life occurs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ce6844ffbe613a48f90", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.9.6", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "death sought but absent", "note": "The commentary says the one in fire neither dies nor lives; Revelation depicts people seeking death without finding it, a close suspended-death image in a different apocalyptic scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9943d1a86284b8bb9e95", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.9.6", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "People seek death but death flees", "note": "In Revelation's torment people long to die yet cannot find death; this is a particularly close formulation beside the commentary's neither-dying-nor-living fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-94757288f9cde9f5bbcc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.12.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A measure of faith allotted by God", "note": "God assigns each person a measure of faith, supplying another specific Christian use of divinely apportioned measure beside the commentary's qadar imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6579d079cb4a0500875a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.7", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "persistent good and immortality", "note": "The commentary says the cultivated field's yield is lasting good; Paul describes those who persist in good work seeking immortality as receiving eternal life, an ethical counterpart to that lasting yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-00c5e07c75cd85112bb0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.18", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "present suffering against future glory", "note": "The commentary contrasts the painful fire of one fate with the better enduring hereafter; Paul says present sufferings are not comparable to the glory to be revealed, giving a Christian formulation of present and future value."}
{"connection_id": "BC-987a5f7a7a7c5b71a3c9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.9.21", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Potter's authority over clay vessels", "note": "Paul uses a potter making vessels for different purposes, joining divine fashioning to differentiated allotment in the way the commentary connects creation and choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-979fe99fb059f62dbca8", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Mortal death and resurrection unto life", "note": "Another brother contrasts death at human hands with hope of resurrection and tells the tyrant he has no resurrection to life; it is a pointed counter-formulation to neither dying nor living in the fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ffdd094b34360db22d3b", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:9", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "A tyrant ends present life, God raises to lasting life", "note": "A tortured brother tells the king that he may dismiss them from present life but the King of the world will raise them to everlasting life, closely meeting Pharaoh's threat and the better enduring life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d277f58127031065253e", "ref": "4 Ezra 14:44", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A restored corpus of revealed books", "note": "Ezra's scribes complete ninety-four books after the law's loss, a Jewish apocalyptic meditation on preserving and differentiating former sacred writings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-98664201ede477b569f8", "ref": "4 Ezra 7:36", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Furnace of torment opposed to a place of rest", "note": "The apocalyptic vision displays the furnace of Gehenna opposite a place of rest, a Jewish eschatological pairing for the commentary's fire and enduring differentiated outcome."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-44622e73abe3380dcac8", "ref": "Apocalypse of Abraham 1–8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham's early confrontation with household idols", "note": "The opening idol episodes are commonly cited for Abraham's testing and rejection of Terah's idols and fire-associated separation; the exact internal sequence should be verified, but it is a specific expansion of the commentary's anti-idolatry Abraham."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c004efb3bc4d5404f139", "ref": "Bava Batra 14b", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Rabbinic attribution of Moses's book", "note": "The Talmud's statement that Moses wrote his book is a Jewish reception of Mosaic textual authority relevant to the commentary's named pages of Moses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e3c8cba6ee0004779357", "ref": "Berakhot 17a", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Righteous enjoying divine radiance in the world to come", "note": "The Talmud's world-to-come scene gives the righteous crowns and enjoyment of divine radiance, a Jewish interpretive counterpart to enduring high degrees rather than temporary worldly life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0985503f2e18e450a21d", "ref": "Exodus Rabbah 2:5", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "bush as Israel under oppression", "note": "The commentary reads Moses' fire as guidance rather than punishment; this midrash reads the unconsumed bush as Israel suffering in Egypt without being destroyed, shifting the sign toward collective endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7175a2d18663aaf0d5bb", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 38:13", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham breaks idols", "note": "The commentary invokes Abraham's request for distance from idols; this midrash's idol-breaking account portrays Abraham actively rejecting his father's idols, making the cited prayer a narrative practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f6d9902af7a963f284d", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 38:13", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham's idol challenge and the furnace", "note": "The midrash has Abraham reject idols and be cast into Nimrod's furnace, a Jewish expansion that tightly joins the commentary's Abrahamic anti-idolatry with its repeated fire scenes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6f7cb6444f11289359f", "ref": "Jubilees 11:16–17", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham's early rejection of idolatry", "note": "These verses are commonly associated with young Abram's recognition of the earth's error in following carved images and his prayer for deliverance; exact wording and division should be verified, but the link is a specific Jewish precursor to the commentary's anti-idolatry Abraham."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0c0acbaa5a8bac0af42f", "ref": "Jubilees 12:1-8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham rejects his father's idols", "note": "The commentary recalls Abraham's plea against idolatry; Jubilees expands his break with his father's idol-making household, giving an early Jewish narrative counterpart to the separation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-18b3259f2d928dbcec0b", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 3:8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Forgetting a word of study", "note": "The Mishnah applies Deuteronomy's warning to the deliberate forgetting of a learned word; it is a Jewish interpretive intensification of the commentary's not-forgetting motif."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39d4dc8f9511f5e2ca60", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:16", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "this world as a vestibule", "note": "The commentary says people prefer present worldly life despite the better hereafter; the Mishnah likens this world to a vestibule before the world to come and urges preparation, turning preference into a practical orientation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9c40eec1c3923bcd727c", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:16", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "This world as corridor before the world to come", "note": "Rabbi Jacob calls this world a vestibule before the world to come, a precise rabbinic frame for the commentary's worldly life versus the later, enduring life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d37ab687f3e71adedc32", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:17", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "good deeds now, bliss hereafter", "note": "The commentary joins cultivated good work to lasting reward and says the hereafter is better than this life; Avot compares one hour of repentance and good deeds here with the world to come, and one hour of its bliss with all this life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-27969b14a5a7f853dd74", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:17", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Good deeds here and bliss in the world to come", "note": "The saying weighs repentance and good deeds in this world together with the incomparable bliss of the world to come, closely mapping the commentary's earthly choice and better permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de9b5c7934b587515c3c", "ref": "Sirach 17:1", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "humanity from earth, days allotted", "note": "The commentary joins bodily creation to divine measuring; Sirach says God made humans from earth and allotted them numbered days, bringing formation and measure together."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d6ac701f40d9748e705d", "ref": "Sirach 2:5", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Gold tested in fire and the righteous in humiliation", "note": "Sirach's fire-testing of gold supplies a Jewish wisdom formulation for the commentary's corrective fire, without making it identical to the punitive fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e0c20631eb015e924c4b", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.36", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Elijah's prayer to the God of Abraham at a fire contest", "note": "Elijah invokes the God of Abraham before the altar fire and asks that Israel know God and his servant; it joins fire, Abraham, divine name, and a public contest of allegiance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a7bb5230f29fb34835b", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.38", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Fire consuming the offering", "note": "The fire of YHWH falls on Elijah's offering, furnishing a Hebrew-Bible fire scene whose purpose is revelation and correction rather than the destruction of a condemned person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dcd81f6d666ce5f5970e", "ref": "WLC:1Kgs.18.39", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "People fall on their faces and confess God", "note": "After the fire the people prostrate themselves and confess YHWH as God, a close narrative meeting of fire, praise, and bowing with the commentary's magicians' prostration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-84fce1503739d514d25b", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.7.1", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Fire descends with divine glory", "note": "At the temple dedication fire descends and God's glory fills the house, a further biblical scene in which fire marks divine presence rather than entry into punitive fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-baa780eb646f840a106d", "ref": "WLC:2Chr.7.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Bowing and praise after the fire", "note": "Seeing the fire and glory, Israel bows and gives thanks for God's enduring steadfast love; it closely combines the commentary's fire, praise, prostration, and permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-862f8f82df2f53548bf6", "ref": "WLC:Amos.3.7", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God reveals a secret to prophetic servants", "note": "Amos says God discloses the divine secret to prophets, a specific prophetic counterpart to the commentary's hidden matter becoming disclosed guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d9b2ab9f2f31925535a", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.10", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Many purified and refined, while the wicked do not understand", "note": "The end-time division has many made pure and refined while the wicked continue blindly; it closely meets the commentary's tazkiya, reminder, and divided response."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81a756c1e995a4c43618", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two outcomes after awakening", "note": "The commentary divides people between fire and purification and contrasts their ultimate fates; Daniel says sleepers in the dust awaken to everlasting life or shame, a specific scriptural division after death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1289c16948d133c9c533", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Everlasting life and everlasting abhorrence", "note": "Daniel's awakening divides people between everlasting life and enduring disgrace, furnishing a biblical future horizon for the commentary's high ranks and great fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d15eddb8f13263229650", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Those guiding many to righteousness shine forever", "note": "The wise shine and those who turn many to righteousness shine like stars forever, a biblical counterpart to guidance, high degrees, and lasting reward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ab4f09b7e0f02e15a165", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.4", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Words shut up and book sealed until the end", "note": "Daniel is told to close the words and seal the book until the time of the end, a direct biblical combination of hidden end-time knowledge and written pages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a3c0cac2ab2b12384cc2", "ref": "WLC:Dan.2.22", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God knows and reveals hidden things", "note": "Daniel says God reveals deep and hidden things and knows what is in darkness, closely meeting the commentary's God who knows what is open and concealed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0f2751761ee8a36f9246", "ref": "WLC:Dan.3.18", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Refusal to worship under a king's fire threat", "note": "The Judeans refuse the king's gods even when a furnace is threatened; the scene closely meets the commentary's prostrating magicians who reject a tyrant's finite authority."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b548e182fd7a1c61c652", "ref": "WLC:Dan.3.25", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "presence in the furnace", "note": "The commentary contrasts punitive fire with the fire at which Moses receives guidance; Daniel's furnace contains a fourth figure beside the three condemned men, making divine presence visible within lethal fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7dc1394083a4de023b9f", "ref": "WLC:Dan.3.25", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Entrants into fire who are unharmed", "note": "The king sees the furnace's entrants walking unhurt; its outcome stands in deliberate tension with the commentary's person who enters the great fire and neither dies nor lives."}
{"connection_id": "BC-99cc0c6655b9c3cac683", "ref": "WLC:Dan.3.27", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unburned in the fire", "note": "The commentary distinguishes one who enters fire from Moses approaching it safely; Daniel's men emerge without bodily harm, a related reversal of fire's destructive action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14485b513e222da6b3bb", "ref": "WLC:Dan.4.17", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Most High gives kingdoms to whom he wills", "note": "Daniel says the Most High rules human kingdoms and gives them as he chooses, directly contesting Pharaoh's self-exalting authority and joining highness to election."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d577ca29c529e059f0e", "ref": "WLC:Dan.4.34", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A humbled king praises the Most High", "note": "After his humiliation, the king blesses and honors the Most High whose dominion endures; it reverses the commentary's tyrannical high claim with praise and lasting rule."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74d90cf34a9311c28b99", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.18", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Holy ones of the Most High possess an everlasting kingdom", "note": "The holy ones of the Most High receive and possess the kingdom forever, a direct biblical meeting of highestness, elevated status, and permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78546336dbe4f77ce3ee", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.27", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Everlasting kingdom given to the people of the Most High", "note": "All dominions serve the holy people whose kingdom endures, answering the commentary's tyrant's temporary rule with lasting high rule under God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-45f0b848d630484218ed", "ref": "WLC:Deut.17.18", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A written Torah copy read throughout life", "note": "The king must write a Torah copy and keep it for continual reading and fear of God, a precise model of page, remembrance, obedience, and durable instruction."}
