{"connection_id": "BC-2f9ab6cc7531256546df", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "choose life", "note": "The commentary contrasts preferring worldly life with the better lasting hereafter; Moses urges Israel to choose life by covenant loyalty, a related choice formula whose immediate setting is life in the land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a1d7849508dcedcbb00", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חַיִּים and ḥayāt", "note": "The commentary repeats Arabic ḥayāt for worldly life; Moses' choice formula uses Hebrew ḥayyîm, a cognate for life, though here it means covenantal life in the land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f70a6efc5eb1bb31fab1", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Choose life", "note": "The call to choose life gives explicit Mosaic form to the commentary's language of preference and its contrast between the near life and what truly endures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f669c7e0f67bd12917b2", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic hayat and Hebrew hayyim in the command to choose life", "note": "Beyond the existing choice motif, the Hebrew command choose hayyim and the commentary's hayat are cognate life terms; the verse grounds its life-choice in an explicit Semitic formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a419f5d773011fdf178b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.10", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seven-year reading cycle", "note": "The commentary describes a reminder publicly offered and recited; Moses sets the law's public reading at the seven-year festival, giving the reading a recurring communal setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1af395d64f6d9297b6f6", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "public reading", "note": "The commentary describes revealed speech being recited and offered as reminder; Moses orders the law read publicly before all Israel, giving a communal scriptural counterpart."}
{"connection_id": "BC-922d098acf2611295a7c", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.12", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hear, learn, fear", "note": "The commentary divides hearers between those who fear and remember and those who turn away; the assembly is to hear the law, learn, and fear the Lord, including children and sojourners."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb00812ff562cd02b9f5", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.13", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fear throughout life", "note": "The commentary's fearful hearer remembers the reminder; Moses says future generations must hear and learn to fear God all their days in the land."}
{"connection_id": "BC-69107ed1675ee733fe66", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Write and teach the song as a witness", "note": "God tells Moses to write a song, teach it, and put it in Israel's mouths; it supplies a Mosaic written-and-remembered form for the commentary's recitation and warning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6a577e6dae29b779b4d", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.21", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A song not forgotten from descendants' mouths", "note": "The divinely given song will not be forgotten from descendants, a particularly close biblical analogue to the commentary's not-forgetting and enduring instruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-19a9e8b2c80524cb5d0b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.24", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses completes writing Torah in a book", "note": "The verse explicitly depicts Moses finishing the words of Torah in a book, the clearest canonical textual counterpart to Moses's pages in 87:19."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1831040555225c0665d6", "ref": "WLC:Deut.31.26", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The Torah book kept as witness", "note": "The written Torah is placed beside the ark as a witness, giving the commentary's named Mosaic pages a preserved and judicial function."}
