{"connection_id": "BC-5bc45b28a3f9cf0cb906", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.15", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Q20:14 li-dhikri and Hebrew zikhri", "note": "At the bush the revealed name is God's perpetual memorial, Hebrew zikhri; its Semitic cognate relation to the commentary's li-dhikri joins divine name and remembrance at the same Moses encounter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81b5e3e2d68d99dbae95", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "burning bush not consumed", "note": "The prose's Moses approaches a fire that gives guidance rather than consuming him; Exodus presents the flame in the bush without consuming it, making the same fire a sign of divine presence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1738e0a5cceef627e4b0", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Moses, fire, and not being consumed", "note": "Exodus makes the burning bush blaze without being consumed; it supplies the same Moses-and-fire encounter against which the commentary distinguishes Moses receiving guidance near fire from the wretched person entering fire in 87:12–13."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fbc724b7acda39c6fd2d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Distant fire becoming a path", "note": "Moses turns aside to inspect the blazing bush, giving a narrative counterpart to the commentary's distant fire that draws him into a way of guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7905d6574a5376f61334", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.4", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses called from the fire", "note": "The commentary places Moses' encounter and instruction at the distant fire; in Exodus the divine voice calls Moses from the bush, so the fire scene becomes a call and response."}

