{"connection_id": "BC-28dfe1f4c11b12029b13", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.5", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Approaching fire as holy ground", "note": "Moses may not approach casually and must remove his sandals because the fire-side ground is holy, adding a concrete bodily response to the commentary's fire that becomes election and worship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ed36ac463635bf8b9e8", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.27", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses commanded to write covenant words", "note": "Moses is told to write the covenant words, another explicit Torah source for the commentary's final appeal to earlier Mosaic pages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f55586fad4a601780eba", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.6", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "name proclaimed to Moses", "note": "The commentary joins prayer and divine-name remembrance to Moses; after Sinai God proclaims the divine name and character before him, giving a second Mosaic setting for that conjunction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ee3abb0567114437308", "ref": "WLC:Exod.34.8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses bows after the name", "note": "The commentary says the person who remembers the Lord's name prays; after the name is proclaimed, Moses bows his head and worships, connecting name-revelation to embodied devotion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d1778909526d56c4a6d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.17", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses's staff for signs", "note": "Moses receives the staff with which he will perform signs, supplying the Torah's concrete bridge from the bush commission to the commentary's staff-and-magicians scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-19ac06e32d00deba8294", "ref": "WLC:Exod.7.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Pharaoh's magicians", "note": "The commentary stresses that Moses' opponents prostrate and reject Pharaoh; Exodus has Pharaoh's magicians practice secret arts for him, so the Qur'anic conversion reverses their role in the parallel contest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b5f7ef08920da14462f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.7.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Pharaoh's Egyptian sorcerers", "note": "Pharaoh summons wise men and sorcerers to meet Moses, the Torah's direct setting for the commentary's later gathering of Moses, Pharaoh, and magicians."}
{"connection_id": "BC-279f6f30dcba798ac502", "ref": "WLC:Exod.7.12", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Competing staffs and Aaron's victory", "note": "The magicians' staffs become serpents but Aaron's staff swallows them; this supplies the contest which the commentary reads through the magicians' eventual refusal of Pharaoh."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6337ad832e9682e485a0", "ref": "WLC:Exod.8.15", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Magicians acknowledge God's agency", "note": "When their art fails, the Egyptian magicians tell Pharaoh that the plague is God's finger; their recognition is a biblical counterpart to the commentary's magicians breaking with Pharaoh's claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e014f96f2a4d10f94094", "ref": "WLC:Exod.8.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "finger of God", "note": "The commentary presents the magicians as turning against Pharaoh and confessing God's better, lasting rule; Exodus's magicians recognize the plague as God's finger but do not renounce Pharaoh, a more limited recognition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a132e36a191bd35cd5f2", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.28.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A ruler claims divinity and is answered that he is mortal", "note": "The ruler of Tyre claims to sit as a god but is told he is human, a close prophetic counter-scene for the commentary's Pharaoh claiming supreme status."}
{"connection_id": "BC-17499e1818d5dbbc3ea9", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.34.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic mar'a and Hebrew mir'eh for pasture", "note": "Ezekiel depicts God feeding Israel in good pasture, Hebrew mir'eh, cognate with the commentary's Arabic mar'a; it turns the shared pasture image toward sustained divine shepherding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a8771a9b69a50920095", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.36.25", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Cleansing from impurities and idols", "note": "God sprinkles clean water, cleanses Israel, and removes its idols, giving the commentary's purification and Abrahamic distance from idolatry a single biblical cleansing horizon."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a33d5c3c4101782e1473", "ref": "WLC:Gen.1.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "vegetation brought forth", "note": "The commentary's pasture is brought out by the creator and then withers; Genesis commands the earth to bring forth seed-bearing plants and trees, grounding vegetation in divine creation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7b63a04121d19c68743", "ref": "WLC:Gen.1.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Earth commanded to bring forth vegetation", "note": "Creation begins with the earth bringing forth seed-bearing vegetation and fruit trees, an immediate Torah counterpart to the commentary's Creator bringing forth pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fcbcc67303cb0b3262ac", "ref": "WLC:Gen.14.18", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Melchizedek as priest of God Most High blessing Abraham", "note": "The priestly blessing names God Most High in an Abraham scene, supplying the canonical context for the highness title already central to the commentary's first and last images."}
{"connection_id": "BC-33b9869149dc8feeb10a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.14.22", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Abraham invokes El Elyon, God Most High", "note": "Abraham names YHWH El Elyon, God Most High; Hebrew elyon and the commentary's al-Ala share the highness root and join the title to Abraham named in 87:19."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16c531ff91b8c37ae7f8", "ref": "WLC:Gen.18.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abraham chosen to command his household in righteousness", "note": "God says Abraham is known so that he will direct his household in God's way of righteousness and justice, adding a canonical Abrahamic election-and-teaching counterpart to the former-pages claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6aeb818ee75d2730927", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.5", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rain, field growth, and a worker for the ground", "note": "Before cultivated plants appear, the text notes both the absence of rain and of a human to work the soil, closely joining the commentary's rain, earth, body, and cultivated field."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ab0a3bad1506e71f73f1", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.7", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "human formed from earth", "note": "The commentary connects divine forming with an embodied creature; Genesis says God formed the human from dust and breathed life into him, a direct creation scene joining body and ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a14252178f95e924a627", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.7", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Human body formed from earth", "note": "God forms the human from ground and breathes life into it, furnishing the body-and-earth side of the commentary's creating and fashioning pair."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e1971cd7ecc5b32fe38", "ref": "WLC:Gen.26.5", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abraham keeps charge, commandments, statutes, and laws", "note": "Genesis attributes comprehensive obedience to Abraham, giving canonical footing for the commentary's Abrahamic former-scripture claim without assuming a canonical Abraham book."}
{"connection_id": "BC-08d9063c3861d13ae74b", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Write the vision so a reader may run", "note": "The vision is written plainly on tablets for a reader in motion, a specific meeting of the commentary's pages, public reminder, and path imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b28eff487f34925f86f0", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A vision for an appointed end that must be awaited", "note": "The vision has an appointed time and may seem slow but will come, a close prophetic analogue to the commentary's concealed decisive hour."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0753d6037155d3863e35", "ref": "WLC:Hos.10.12", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "breaking fallow ground", "note": "The commentary interprets success through a farmer's work, with a cultivated field yielding lasting good; Hosea commands sowing righteousness, reaping steadfast love, and breaking fallow ground until God sends righteousness like rain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f34e4e0114d0c6c77945", "ref": "WLC:Hos.10.12", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sow righteousness and break fallow ground", "note": "Hosea links cultivated ground, righteousness, and a coming divine response, a close ethical field analogue to the commentary's falah-like salvation and lasting crop."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34b0f7a46395e2490495", "ref": "WLC:Isa.14.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "A ruler aspiring to be like the Most High", "note": "The arrogant ruler seeks equality with the Most High, a non-Pharaoh biblical analogue for the commentary's additional Pharaoh claim to supreme height."}
{"connection_id": "BC-464418a48b023206c907", "ref": "WLC:Isa.17.13", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Roaring waters and dispersed chaff", "note": "Nations roar like waters but flee like chaff before wind; this joins rushing flood imagery to transient residue as the commentary does."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d164aa8e22f29001b3f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.28.17", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Floodwaters sweep away a false refuge", "note": "Hail and overflowing waters expose a refuge made of falsehood, a prophetic water judgment that complements the commentary's flood separating what vanishes from what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dbaa7bec4271c2b3a73d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.12", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God measures the waters and creation", "note": "Isaiah pictures God measuring waters in his hand and the cosmos by span, expanding the commentary's measuring scene from floodwater to ordered creation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eb9d9ec6ae66a40b8d6c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.6", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human life as grass", "note": "The commentary says the preferred worldly life is the same object as the withering pasture; Isaiah declares all flesh grass, turning that image directly toward human transience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8cde3a3fbf0260c26317", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers", "note": "The commentary describes pasture drying before it is swept away; Isaiah says grass withers when the Lord's breath blows on it, giving decay a divine frame."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a08e6f89df6b35269e82", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Grass withers and flower fades", "note": "Isaiah's grass and flower wither under divine breath, giving a close biblical form to the commentary's worldly life becoming dried pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-900917e105f118866b9f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word endures", "note": "The commentary contrasts transient worldly life with what is better and more lasting; Isaiah's grass fades but God's word stands forever, adding a specific enduring term to the contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0e49f3458d25e6343bb3", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's word stands forever after grass withers", "note": "The word's permanence is set directly against withering grass, completing the commentary's contrast between transient vegetation and what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b73c74b6faadb7ec765b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.41.8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abraham named God's friend and chosen lineage", "note": "Isaiah calls Israel the offspring of Abraham God's friend and the chosen servant, supporting the commentary's pairing of Abraham, election, and earlier divine promise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5792e3a5acd1c98d9307", "ref": "WLC:Isa.42.16", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God guides the blind on an unknown path", "note": "God leads blind people on paths they do not know and turns darkness into light, a close biblical formulation for the commentary's measure-then-guidance and fire-path scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5d3974dc9f209da598da", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Maker from the womb who has chosen", "note": "Isaiah calls God the maker and former from the womb and names the chosen servant, an unusually close pairing for the commentary's crafted body and selection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a9c52b8a8f478508642c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.24", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "formed from the womb and creator", "note": "The commentary moves from bodily formation to the Lord's work in creation; Isaiah calls God the one who formed the speaker from the womb and stretched out the heavens, placing those acts under one creator."}

