{"connection_id": "BC-fac364bbdceea5ff8d33", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.4", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Growth like grass beside watercourses", "note": "The renewed descendants spring up among grass like trees by water, a close prophetic image for the commentary's watered growth instead of dried refuse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-119b055f3936beb76e14", "ref": "WLC:Isa.48.10", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Refined in the furnace of affliction", "note": "God refines Israel in an affliction-furnace, an explicit prophetic development of the commentary's fire as corrective work rather than only punishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0394575904a15ddb5c55", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The servant made a polished arrow", "note": "Isaiah's servant is made into a polished arrow and kept hidden; this supplies the crafted-arrow secondary sense which the commentary finds in the creation verb."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c27570539de8959bcd3b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.5.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Prepared vineyard yielding wild grapes", "note": "The carefully cultivated vineyard gives wild fruit instead of the expected crop; it adds a prophetic moral version of the commentary's contrast between worked land and uncontrolled growth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7851b892203b6d36c4d1", "ref": "WLC:Isa.5.24", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Fire consumes stubble because teaching is rejected", "note": "Fire devours stubble and dry grass because people reject God's teaching, a very close prophetic fusion of dried vegetation, fire, and refusal of reminder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5aefa114bb9291427e7c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.51.6", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Created things wear out but salvation lasts forever", "note": "Heavens vanish, earth wears out, and inhabitants die, while God's salvation and righteousness endure; this closely matches the commentary's temporary worldly life and better permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72936c9b0616d7329cc6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.10", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain yields seed and bread", "note": "The commentary follows water into pasture and distinguishes refuse from useful yield; Isaiah says rain waters earth so it sprouts and provides seed and bread, an especially close image of water becoming useful produce."}
{"connection_id": "BC-774d317db3b4262ecd1c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.10", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rain makes earth sprout and yield", "note": "Rain descends, waters the earth, and makes it bear seed and bread; it is the biblical side of the commentary's rain, pasture, cultivated field, and beneficial yield."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6f61fd9339f4f3cfbb56", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word does not return empty", "note": "The commentary joins the rain's useful remainder to the offered reminder; Isaiah applies rain's productive return to God's word, which accomplishes its purpose rather than returning empty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89430d1b8ba6021dc29e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.57.15", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The high and lofty one revives the lowly", "note": "The high and lofty God dwells with the contrite in order to revive them, pairing the commentary's highness with its purified, responsive person rather than the arrogant ruler."}
{"connection_id": "BC-adc828c08fc240e995b8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.57.20", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Restless waters casting up mud", "note": "The wicked are like a turbulent sea that throws up mire, a related prophetic water-and-waste image beside the commentary's flood-borne scum; the material correspondence is indirect."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fef8b654f62ddf981aac", "ref": "WLC:Isa.61.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Earth and garden making righteousness and praise sprout", "note": "As earth makes sprouts grow, God makes righteousness and praise grow, joining the commentary's cultivated field to purification and praise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e56422329fa24f38317", "ref": "WLC:Isa.64.8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Clay, potter, and work of God's hand", "note": "The potter-clay image gives the commentary's craft language a biblical body-and-maker analogy without reducing it to a lexical echo."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2b9f8134f90b8045600", "ref": "WLC:Isa.66.24", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Undying worm and unquenched fire", "note": "Isaiah's final spectacle of the unquenched fire and worm that does not die is a close Hebrew-Bible horizon for the commentary's great fire and suspended death-life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70abba2c7ce163438dbf", "ref": "WLC:Jer.1.5", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "formed and chosen", "note": "The commentary pairs the shaped creature with Moses' selection; Jeremiah says God knew and consecrated him before forming him in the womb, joining bodily formation to prophetic choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14dac58c37fbe80986a5", "ref": "WLC:Jer.1.5", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Formed in the womb and appointed", "note": "Before forming Jeremiah in the womb God knows, consecrates, and appoints him; it joins formation, election, and a divine mission as the commentary does at Moses's fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8da98aee5d6e16c54eeb", "ref": "WLC:Jer.17.6", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shrub in the dry desert", "note": "The commentary contrasts untended growth that dries with cultivated yield; Jeremiah depicts the one who trusts in human strength as a desert shrub that does not see good, giving barrenness an ethical cause."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce67ae17892b661d09f5", "ref": "WLC:Jer.17.8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree by water keeps yielding", "note": "The commentary says the cultivated field grows and leaves a lasting share; Jeremiah's water-side tree does not cease yielding fruit even in drought, making trust the basis of endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a07b23f2ca94bc5dfac0", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.24", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "No one can hide beyond God's sight", "note": "The prophet asks whether anyone can hide in secret places from the God who fills heaven and earth, closely matching the commentary's claim of divine knowledge of the hidden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-259fedaf76f904ec7345", "ref": "WLC:Jer.23.29", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's word like fire", "note": "The divine word is likened to fire and a hammer, supporting the commentary's movement from recited reminder to a fire that actively corrects."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4c6d8632c4ce4044b1f", "ref": "WLC:Jer.4.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Break fallow ground and do not sow among thorns", "note": "Jeremiah turns cultivation into moral exhortation, making a direct Jewish parallel for the commentary's ploughed field versus vegetation left to become waste."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f48db81ced0e2cdd06d2", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.10", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "body formed in the womb", "note": "The commentary's shaping image includes the embryo; Job likens God's formation of his body to milk poured and curdled, a concrete image of prenatal making."}
{"connection_id": "BC-525d40f66a082b8ae9c0", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.11", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Skin, flesh, bones, and sinews fashioned", "note": "Job describes God clothing and knitting the body, a concrete biblical counterpart to the commentary's secondary sense of bodily and embryonic formation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1bfb3dbfd395f3eb665f", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.19", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Floodwater washes away earth", "note": "Water wears stone and sweeps away soil, a literal counterpart to the commentary's flood carrying off what has dried and become refuse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc1d54b777627b01580e", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life as a fading flower", "note": "The commentary says worldly life is also pictured as a flower and does not last; Job says a human comes forth like a flower and withers, a direct human-life use of that image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81c1783e728ae3760294", "ref": "WLC:Job.15.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Arabic tazakka and Hebrew yizkeh around purity", "note": "Job asks how a human, including one born of woman, can be pure, using Hebrew yizkeh from the z-k-y root cognate with the commentary's tazakka; its setting adds bodily finitude to purification."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac89362562f6811eef37", "ref": "WLC:Job.28.25", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Waters apportioned by measure", "note": "Job says God established weight for wind and measured the waters, closely meeting the commentary's flood running in valleys according to measure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c77792f418f7a075ce5", "ref": "WLC:Job.29.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Walking through darkness by God's lamp", "note": "Job recalls walking through darkness by God's shining lamp, a concrete light-and-way image alongside the commentary's fire sought for a guide."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ba894a7ef861735a44d", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.25", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "channels for rain", "note": "The commentary links measured water in valleys to divine allotment; God asks who cut a channel for torrents of rain, a precise image of water given a course."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0a016401f8f64b4667e2", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.26", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rain in uncultivated land", "note": "The commentary joins the valley flood to pasture; Job asks who sends rain on land where no one lives, emphasizing provision beyond human fields."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aff5df9659c1906005dd", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.27", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass sprouts", "note": "The commentary traces water to pasture's growth; Job says rain makes the wilderness satisfied and causes grass to sprout, completing that water-to-vegetation sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28ec3b6d59cc478ac43d", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.27", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Rain makes grass sprout in waste ground", "note": "God's rain satisfies desolate ground and causes grass to spring up, a precise meeting of the commentary's rain-shaken earth and brought-forth pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d231ab5e4d2f11fcd30", "ref": "WLC:Job.8.12", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Green marsh plant withering before other growth", "note": "A water-dependent plant withers while still green, a specific wisdom image for the commentary's pasture turning into waste; the water setting differs from its valley flood."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76acab113c429f726d23", "ref": "WLC:Josh.1.8", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Book in the mouth and continual meditation", "note": "Joshua is told not to let the Torah book depart from his mouth but to meditate on it day and night, matching the commentary's preserved speech, remembrance, and practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6a69a4b933d461a4a38", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.14", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Put away foreign gods and serve YHWH", "note": "In the Abrahamic ancestral context Joshua calls Israel to remove foreign gods and serve YHWH, a direct biblical counterpart to the commentary's avoidance of idols."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6ddf229a5892781e1e0d", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.15", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Choose whom you will serve", "note": "Joshua's demand to choose service of YHWH or other gods makes the commentary's preference theme concrete at the point of idolatry."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92355f913669d99f3395", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abraham's family and other gods", "note": "The commentary closes by recalling Abraham's plea to be kept from idols; Joshua says Abraham's ancestors served other gods beyond the River, supplying the family setting from which that plea distances him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f448f69948176dbc65d9", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.2", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Abraham's ancestors serve other gods", "note": "Joshua locates Abraham's family beyond the river among worshippers of other gods, supplying canonical background for the commentary's Abrahamic plea to be kept from idols."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a67f082e388d677658b", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Abraham taken from beyond the River", "note": "The commentary names Abraham among the recipients of the earlier pages and recalls his separation from idols; Joshua says God took Abraham from beyond the River and led him through Canaan, giving a scriptural account of his departure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb383ad7fe3b0445422b", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.3", "target": "sec18", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God takes and leads Abraham", "note": "God takes Abraham from beyond the river and leads him through Canaan, matching the commentary's Abrahamic selection and its wider path-and-guidance movement."}

